{{also|Withers}}
==English==

===Etymology===
1580, from {{etyl|ang|en}} dialectal {{m|ang|wiðer||against}} (cf. ''[[wither-]]'') {{suffix||s|lang=en}}; see {{m|en|with}}. So-named because the part of the horse that pushes ''against'' a load. Compare {{cog|de|Widerrist||withers}}, from {{m|de|wider||against}} + {{m|de|Rist||wrist}}.<ref>{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|/ˈwɪð.ɚz/|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈwɪð.əz/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|with|ers|lang=en}}
{{rfap|lang=en}}
 [[Category:English 2-syllable words]]

===Noun===
{{en-plural noun}}

# {{lb|en|veterinary medicine}} The part of the [[back]] of a [[four-legged]] [[animal]] that is between the [[shoulder blades]]; in many species the [[highest]] point of the [[body]] and the standard place to measure the animal's height.

====Usage notes====
Although this noun refers to one object, there is no corresponding singular form *{{term||wither}} for this word, the singular form being obsolete.

